Q: C++. 1 same object that can be one of multiple classes instances. From same object, calling a method present in 2+ classes with same name &amp; interface A: <p>That being said, I gather you want something non-intrusive. You don't want to make the classes inherit from a common base class but they do share the same interface. You want to be able to swap objects of the classes at runtime, hence using just templates won't help. You need some sort of type erasure. That could be a case for <code>std::variant</code>:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;variant&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

struct A { void foo() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;A&quot;;}};
struct B { void foo() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;B&quot;;}};

int main() {
    std::variant&lt;A,B&gt; ab;
    ab = A();
    std::visit([](auto&amp; x){x.foo();},ab);
    ab = B();
    std::visit([](auto&amp; x){x.foo();},ab);
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/oGTWhMe1c" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Output</a>:</p>
<pre><code>AB
</code></pre>
<p>Note that there is no way to write the above in a C++/Python-agnostic way. Python has no need for <code>std::variant</code>, nor does C++ do dynamic typing as Python does.</p>

Q: libuv: uv_check_t and uv_prepare_t usage A: <p>Albeit more than a decade after the question: I wonder if these might be in any ways similar to the <a href="https://github.com/protowire-hub/libev/wiki/ev(3)#ev_prepare-and-ev_check---customise-your-event-loop" rel="nofollow noreferrer">check and prepare </a><em><a href="https://github.com/protowire-hub/libev/wiki/ev(3)#ev_prepare-and-ev_check---customise-your-event-loop" rel="nofollow noreferrer">watchers</a></em><a href="https://github.com/protowire-hub/libev/wiki/ev(3)#ev_prepare-and-ev_check---customise-your-event-loop" rel="nofollow noreferrer"> in libev</a>? From the <a href="https://github.com/protowire-hub/libev/wiki/ev(3)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ev(3) manual page</a>, insofar as for with regards to check and prepar watchers in libev:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and check watchers are often (but not always) used in pairs: prepare watchers get invoked before the process blocks and check watchers afterwards.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Albeit, some limitations from libev might not apply in libuv, such as from ev(3):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You must not call ev_run (or similar functions that enter the current event loop) or ev_loop_fork from either ev_prepare or ev_check watchers. Other loops than the current one are fine, however. The rationale behind this is that you do not need to check for recursion in those watchers, i.e. the sequence will always be ev_prepare, blocking, ev_check so if you have one watcher of each kind they will always be called in pairs bracketing the blocking call.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Update: Within the contemporary reference documentation for libuv, there's a description of each of the <a href="https://docs.libuv.org/en/v1.x/prepare.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>uv_prepare_t</code></a> and <a href="https://docs.libuv.org/en/v1.x/check.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>uv_check_t</code></a> <em>handle</em> types. Quoting same:</p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;Prepare handles will run the given callback once per loop iteration, right before polling for i/o.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Check handles will run the given callback once per loop iteration, right after polling for i/o.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>Insofar as for examples in C++, perhaps one could offer an example using the <a href="https://github.com/skypjack/uvw" rel="nofollow noreferrer">uvw</a>\[1\] interface for libuv in C++.</p>
<p>For what it's worth, this would also include an illustration of using the <em>user data</em> feature, as available for loops and for handler objects in libuv. This uses the shared pointer interface for user data in <a href="https://github.com/skypjack/uvw" rel="nofollow noreferrer">uvw</a>.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;
#include &lt;uvw/loop.h&gt;
#include &lt;uvw/check.h&gt;


inline void thunk() {
    std::shared_ptr&lt;uvw::loop&gt; loop = uvw::loop::get_default();
    // create the check_handle
    auto check = loop-&gt;resource&lt;uvw::check_handle&gt;();

    // types for user data
    using record_type = std::vector&lt;std::size_t&gt;;
    using info_type = std::tuple&lt;std::size_t, std::size_t, record_type&gt;;

    {
        // initialize user data for the check_handle
        record_type record_prototype{};
        record_prototype.reserve(10U);
        check-&gt;data(std::make_shared&lt;info_type&gt;(0U, 10U, std::move(record_prototype)));
    }

    check-&gt;on&lt;uvw::error_event&gt;(
          [](uvw::error_event const&amp; event, uvw::check_handle&amp; handle) {
              std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;Error: &quot; &lt;&lt; event.what();
          });

    check-&gt;on&lt;uvw::check_event&gt;([](uvw::check_event const&amp;, uvw::check_handle&amp; hdl) {
        std::shared_ptr&lt;info_type&gt; info = hdl.data&lt;info_type&gt;();
        assert(info);
        // check the current iteration count. increment if not at limit
        if (std::get&lt;0&gt;(*info) &lt; std::get&lt;1&gt;(*info)) {
            // data recording for purpose of test
            record_type&amp; rec = std::get&lt;2&gt;(*info);
            rec.emplace_back(std::get&lt;0&gt;(*info));

            std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;COUNT++ : &quot; &lt;&lt; std::get&lt;0&gt;(*info) &lt;&lt; std::endl;
            std::get&lt;0&gt;(*info)++;
            // run the loop with the nowait flag, again
            hdl.parent().run(uvw::loop::run_mode::NOWAIT);
        } else {
            std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;COUNT FINAL&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
            hdl.stop();
        }
    });


    assert(check-&gt;start() == 0);
    assert(check-&gt;active());
    assert(!check-&gt;closing());

    // using the nowait flag as to ensure
    // that this call will run the loop 
    // at most once
    loop-&gt;run(uvw::loop::run_mode::NOWAIT);

    record_type&amp; rec = std::get&lt;2&gt;(*check-&gt;data&lt;info_type&gt;());
    assert(rec.size() == 10U);
    for(std::size_t n = 0U; n &lt; 10U; n++) {
        assert(rec.at(n) == n);
    }
}

int main() {
    thunk();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If it could help to clarify the application above:</p>
<ul>
<li>if <code>loop-&gt;run()</code> would be called without the nowait flag then this would run indefinitely. Given the set of exactly one uv handle as a check handle, throughout the lifetime of this loop, It may seem that  the check watcher's callback would not ever be activated if the loop was run with default flags.</li>
<li>Corresponding with the initial run(nowait) call, the check handle's callback itself will run the loop again - using the nowait flag - until the count is reached.</li>
<li>As a known limitation, this has not been tested with relation to any or other handle types in the libuv/uvw.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <a href="https://github.com/skypjack/uvw/tree/main/test/uvw" rel="nofollow noreferrer">uvw tests</a> might include an example application for each available <a href="https://docs.libuv.org/en/v1.x/handle.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">libuv handle type</a>, as interfaced via <a href="https://github.com/skypjack/uvw" rel="nofollow noreferrer">uvw</a>. The above example was developed after these tests</p>
<p>[1]  If one might be using clang on a Linux host, libuv and uvw were both compiled using gcc here</p>

Q: Select2 upon click(not selected), show loading spinner javascript A: <p>I have a solution on <strong>select2</strong> dropdown loading spinner for <strong>Ajax</strong> calls.. 
With 2 simple functions, we will place the loading icon in the dropdowns</p>

<p><strong>Add Referances</strong> (select2 dropdown cdn)</p>

<pre><code>  &lt;link href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/select2/4.0.6-rc.0/css/select2.min.css" rel="stylesheet" /&gt;
  &lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/select2/4.0.6-rc.0/js/select2.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

<p></p>



<p><strong>Html</strong></p>

<pre><code>div class="row"&gt;
&lt;div class="col-md-4"&gt;
    &lt;select class="js-example-basic-single" name="state" id="country"&gt;
        &lt;option value="AL"&gt;Alabama&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="WY"&gt;Wyoming&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="ZB"&gt;Zimbamwe&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="TR"&gt;Türkiye&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;/select&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="col-md-4"&gt;
    &lt;select class="js-example-basic-single" name="state" id="city"&gt;&lt;/select&gt;        
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="col-md-4"&gt;
    &lt;select class="js-example-basic-single" name="state" id="district"&gt;&lt;/select&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>

<p></p>

<p><strong>addLoadSpiner</strong></p>

<p>This function use to add or show spinner gif on dropdown. The loading spinner is dynamically created as an html element and added to the dropdown. if already added before, just displayed on screen.</p>

<pre><code>  function addLoadSpiner(el) {
        debugger;
        if (el.length &gt; 0) {
            if ($("#img_" + el[0].id).length &gt; 0) {
                $("#img_" + el[0].id).css('display', 'block');
            }               
            else {
                var img = $('&lt;img class="ddloading"&gt;');
                img.attr('id', "img_" + el[0].id);
                img.attr('src', 'http://www.lettersmarket.com/uploads/lettersmarket/blog/loaders/common_gray/ajax_loader_gray_512.gif');
                img.css({ 'display': 'block', 'width': '25px', 'height': '25px', 'z-index': '100', 'float': 'right' });
                img.prependTo(el[0].nextElementSibling);
            }
            el.prop("disabled", true);               
        }
    }
</code></pre>

<p><strong>hideLoadSpinner</strong></p>

<p>Add css <code>css('display', 'none')</code> is removed from the screen</p>

<pre><code>function hideLoadSpinner(el) {
        if (el.length &gt; 0) {
            if ($("#img_" + el[0].id).length &gt; 0) {
                 setTimeout(function () {
                     $("#img_" + el[0].id).css('display', 'none');
                     el.prop("disabled", false);
                }, 500);                    
            }
        }
    }
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Load Data With Ajax</strong></p>

<p>Finally , Loading spinner appears on the display until Ajax has finished</p>

<pre><code>        $.ajax({
            url: "/Home/GetCities",
            data: JSON.stringify({
                                  'countryId': $("#country option:selected").val()
                                }),
            type: 'POST',
            cache: false,
            contentType: 'application/json; charset=utf-8',
            dataType: "json",
            success: function (result) {
                var dbSelect = $('#city');

                addLoadSpiner(dbSelect);     

                dbSelect.empty();
                $.each(result, function (index, item) {
                    dbSelect.append("&lt;option value='" + item.id + "'&gt;" + item.text + "&lt;/option&gt;")
                });

                hideLoadSpinner(dbSelect);

            },
            error: function (xhr, ajaxOptions, thrownError) {
                alert(thrownError);                  
            }
        });
</code></pre>

Q: How to Add header row in text file using java A: <p>Read the file, add the header and save the file:</p>

<pre><code>try{
    BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(yourFile));
    String header = "Your Header";
    String content = "";
    while (in.ready()) {
      content += in.readLine();
    }
    in.close();

    String output = header + System.getProperty("line.separator") + content;

    FileWriter fstream = new FileWriter(YourOutputFile);
    BufferedWriter out = new BufferedWriter(fstream);
    out.write(output);  
    out.close();

  }catch (IOException e){
    System.err.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
  }
</code></pre>

Q: PHP PDO - There is no active transaction A: <p>Wrap your transaction code inside a try-catch statement.</p>
<pre><code>$tags_input = array(6,4,5);
$conn = new PDO('mysql:host='.DB_HOST.';dbname='.DB_NAME.';charset=utf8', DB_USER, DB_PASSW, array(  
    PDO::ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES =&gt; false,  
    PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE =&gt; PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION,PDO::MYSQL_ATTR_INIT_COMMAND =&gt; &quot;SET NAMES 'utf8'&quot;)
);

try {  
    $conn-&gt;beginTransaction();   
    $sql = &quot;INSERT INTO projects (id, pr_id, enabled) VALUES ( :val0, :val1, :val2)&quot;;
    $stmt = $conn-&gt;prepare($sql);  
    if(count($tags_input)&gt;0){
            for($i = 0;$i&lt;count($tags_input);$i++){
                    $stmt-&gt;bindValue(':val0', 57); 
                    $stmt-&gt;bindValue(':val1', $tags_input[$i]); 
                    $stmt-&gt;bindValue(':val2', 'Y'); 
                    $result = $stmt-&gt;execute();
            }
    }
    $res1 = $conn-&gt;commit();    
} catch (Exception $e) {
    $conn-&gt;rollBack();
    throw $e;
}
</code></pre>
<p>A really well-based and straightforward explanation of the answer was provided by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/1864166/richard-duerr">Richard</a> as a comment.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The reason you got error is because you were trying to close a transaction when it was already closed. <strong>beginTransaction</strong> opens one, and EITHER <strong>rollBack</strong> OR <strong>commit</strong> closes it. You have to avoid doing BOTH actions, meaning commit/rollback, for a single <strong>beginTransaction</strong> statement, or you'll get an error. The above try/catch code ensures that only one closing statement is executed.</p>
</blockquote>

Q: How do I secure express app with better-auth A: <p>Thanks for the tips! I still find this a bit confusing. After a few attempts, I came up with the following setup.</p>
<p>I’m using <code>cookie-session</code> middleware with Express:</p>
<pre><code>server.setConfig((app) =&gt; {
  app.use(express.json());
  app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));

  app.use(cookieParser());
  app.use(
    cookieSession({
      name: 'session',
      keys: [process.env.COOKIE_SESSION_SECRET],
      maxAge: 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000,
    })
  );

  // [...] rest of the setup
  app.user(requireAuth) // custom middleware to protect all routes in app
});
</code></pre>
<p>Better-auth instance and configuration:</p>
<pre><code>import { betterAuth } from 'better-auth';
import { Pool } from 'pg';

export const auth = betterAuth({
  emailAndPassword: {
    enabled: true
  },
  database: new Pool({
    connectionString: process.env.AUTH_DATABASE_URL
  })
});
</code></pre>
<p>My current login endpoint (for now using a hardcoded username and password) looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>@httpPost('/login')
public async submitLogin(@request() req: Request, @response() _res: Response) {
  const response = await auth.api.signInEmail({
    body: {
      email: 'john.doe@example.com', // required
      password: 'password1234',      // required
    },
  });

  req.session.user = response.user;

  return this.redirect('/');
}
</code></pre>
<p>And here’s the middleware I’m using to protect routes:</p>
<pre><code>export async function requireAuth(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) {
  if (req.path.startsWith('/auth')) return next();

  if (!req.session.user) {
    return res.redirect('/auth/login');
  }

  next();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Does this look like a proper and secure way to use Better Auth? It feels a little bit over the top.</p>
